[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Atomic Operations with Batched Writes in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building modern web and mobile applications, ensuring data integrity is crucial. In Firebase Firestore, batched writes allow you to execute multiple write operations as a single atomic unit. This tutorial covers how to implement batched writes efficiently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Batched Writes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A batched write contains multiple set, update, or delete operations. Unlike standard individual writes, a batch guarantees that either all operations in the batch succeed, or none of them are applied to the database. This prevents partial updates that can corrupt your data state."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Batches can contain up to 500 operations maximum."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operations inside a batch do not read data; they only write."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You are billed for each operation included in the batch individually."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Batched Write in JavaScript"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following code example demonstrates how to initialize a write batch, queue multiple operations (such as setting a document, updating another, and deleting a third), and commit the batch."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import { initializeApp } from "firebase/app";
import { getFirestore, writeBatch, doc } from "firebase/firestore";

const firebaseConfig = { apiKey: "YOUR_API_KEY", projectId: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID" };
const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
const db = getFirestore(app);

async function executeBatchUpdate() {
  const batch = writeBatch(db);

  const sfRef = doc(db, "cities", "SF");
  batch.set(sfRef, { name: "San Francisco", state: "CA", country: "USA" });

  const laRef = doc(db, "cities", "LA");
  batch.update(laRef, { population: 3900000 });

  const nycRef = doc(db, "cities", "NYC");
  batch.delete(nycRef);

  await batch.commit();
  console.log("Batch executed successfully!");
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Limitations"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While batched writes are powerful, keep in mind that they are not a substitute for distributed transactions where you need to read data before deciding how to write it. If you need conditional reads, use Firestore transactions instead."}]
